t":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIf $(2x+5)(x-3)=14$, find the sum of the possible values of $x$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"c8616e4f87152d1edda0b753e09c187f871e6c4ca5d9c521ed0eb3afbea94525"},"seconds":9.577822208404541,"seed":1054051079143538356,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the equation \\((2x+5)(x-3)=14\\) and find the sum of the possible values of \\(x\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Expand the left-hand side of the equation.\n2. Move the constant term on the right-hand side to the left-hand side to form a standard quadratic equation.\n3. Identify the coefficients of the quadratic equation.\n4. Use the relationship between the roots of a quadratic equation to find the sum of the roots.\n\nLet's start with the given equation:\n\\[\n(2x+5)(x-3) = 14\n\\]\n\nFirst, expand the left-hand side:\n\\[\n2x^2 + 5x - 6x - 15 = 14\n\\]\n\\[\n2x^2 - x - 15 = 14\n\\]\n\nNext, move the constant term on the right-hand side to the left-hand side:\n\\[\n2x^2 - x - 15 - 14 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n2x^2 - x - 29 = 0\n\\]\n\nNow we have a standard quadratic equation of the form \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -1\\), and \\(c = -29\\).\n\nThe sum of the roots of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) is given by \\(-\\frac{b}{a}\\). Here, \\(a = 2\\) and \\(b = -1\\), so the sum of the roots is:\n\\[\n-\\frac{-1}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the possible values of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:26"}
